+# Contract: HTTP API (feature 002 delta)
+
+**Feature**: `002-burn-after-reading-password` | **Plan**: [../plan.md](../plan.md)
+
+This file states **only what changes**. Feature 001's contract,
+[../../001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md](../../001-pastebin-service/contracts/http-api.md),
+remains in force in full: the same four requests, the same paths and methods, the same 404 for every
+other path and method, the same `{"error": "..."}` body on every unsuccessful response, the same
+timestamp format, the same accurate `Content-Length`, HTTP/1.0 with one request per connection, and
+`127.0.0.1` only. Two request fields, one request header, and two response fields are added; one
+numeric bound grows. Nothing is removed and nothing changes meaning (FR-068).
+
+Field names, the header name, and the status codes below are fixed by the spec's Constraints section
+and are not implementation choices.
+
+## `POST /pastes` — two added request fields
+
+| Field | Type | Required | Meaning |
+|-------|------|----------|---------|
+| `burn_after_reading` | boolean | no | `true` makes the snippet one-time: its first successful read is its last. Absent means `false`. |
+| `password` | string | no | 1 to 128 characters, **counted in characters**, not encoded bytes. Reading the snippet then requires `X-Paste-Password`. Absent means the snippet is unprotected. |
+
+**`null` is not an accepted value for either field.** This is deliberately unlike `expires_in`,
+where `null` means "no expiry": absence, not `null`, is how a caller declines either option
+(FR-046, FR-055).
+
+No coercion of any kind. `"true"`, `"false"`, `1`, `0`, `[]`, `{}`, and `null` are each a `400` for
+`burn_after_reading`; `""`, a 129-character string, a number, a boolean, `null`, `[]`, and `{}` are
+each a `400` for `password`. A rejected publication creates nothing.
+
+Unknown fields are still ignored, including alongside these two (FR-068). `Content-Type` is still
+neither required nor consulted.
+
+The `201` response is **unchanged** — `id`, `delete_token`, `url` — and in particular never carries
+the password or anything derived from it (FR-057).
+
+Added failures: `400` (`burn_after_reading` not a boolean; `password` not a string of 1–128
+characters).
+
+## `X-Paste-Password` — the read-time request header
+
+Carried on `GET /pastes/<id>` and `GET /pastes/<id>/raw`.
+
+| Condition | Treatment |
+|-----------|-----------|
+| The snippet is **not** protected | The header is **not inspected at all** — the read succeeds whatever the header's value or bytes, including bytes that are not valid UTF-8 and including the header given more than once (FR-062). |
+| The snippet is protected, header present exactly once, bytes decode as UTF-8 to the snippet's password | The read proceeds exactly as for an unprotected snippet. |
+| The snippet is protected, header absent | `403` |
+| The snippet is protected, header present exactly once, value does not match | `403` |
+| The snippet is protected, header present exactly once, value is empty | `403` — an empty value is a presented password of zero characters, which can match nothing, so it is refused rather than treated as absent. |
+| The snippet is protected, header present exactly once, bytes are not valid UTF-8 | `403` — never a `500` (FR-070). |
+| The snippet is protected, header present **more than once** | `403`, with **no occurrence selected** — not the first, not the last, not any. A correct password must be presented exactly once to be accepted (FR-072). |
+
+The header's bytes are interpreted as **UTF-8**, so a password containing non-ASCII characters is
+presentable and matchable. Comparison is exact: no trimming, no case folding, no Unicode
+normalisation (FR-058). It does not reveal through timing how much of a presented password was
+correct (FR-061).
+
+The four `403` rows are one outcome with one message; they are indistinguishable to the caller by
+design.
+
+## `GET /pastes/<id>` — two added response fields
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg",
+  "content": "hello",
+  "created_at": "2026-10-23T20:15:00Z",
+  "expires_at": null,
+  "burn_after_reading": false,
+  "protected": false
+}
+```
+
+`burn_after_reading` and `protected` are JSON booleans, always present and never `null`, for every
+snippet including one stored by the previous version of the service (FR-064, FR-067). The four
+existing fields are unchanged in name, type, order of meaning, and value. `delete_token` is still
+absent, and the password — in any form, derived or otherwise — is never present (FR-066).
+
+If the snippet is one-time, this read consumed it: every later request for the identifier, through
+either endpoint, is a `404`.
+
+Added failures: `403` (protected; see the header table above).
+
+## `GET /pastes/<id>/raw` — unchanged
+
+`200` with the snippet's text as the entire body, `Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8`, no
+wrapper and no added newline. **No flag, field, or header is added to this response** (FR-065).
+
+If the snippet is one-time, this read consumed it, exactly as the structured read does.
+
+Added failures: `403` (protected; see the header table above).
+
+## `DELETE /pastes/<id>` — unchanged
+
+Still `X-Delete-Token` and nothing else. A password neither substitutes for the delete token nor is
+required in addition to it, and the header is not consulted on this endpoint at all (FR-063).
+
+An unread one-time snippet deletes normally and returns `204`; an already-consumed one returns `404`,
+as any gone snippet does (FR-052).
+
+## Precedence among failures on a read
+
+Evaluated in this order, so that each row outranks every row below it:
+
+1. **`404`** — the identifier is unknown, malformed, deleted, expired, or already consumed. This
+   outranks `403`: no password, correct or otherwise, can turn it into any other status, and the
+   endpoint reveals nothing a read would not already have revealed (FR-060).
+2. **`500`** — the stored record exists but is damaged. Still an internal error, never a `404` or a
+   `403`, and a damaged one-time snippet is not consumed (FR-049).
+3. **`403`** — the snippet is protected and the presented password does not match.
+4. **`200`** — and only here, if the snippet is one-time, is it consumed.
+
+A one-time snippet is taken out of circulation, durably, *before* the first byte of its response is
+written. A caller that abandons the connection mid-body, or a service stopped at that instant, leaves
+the snippet consumed and gone rather than readable again (FR-048, FR-051).
+
+When several reads of the same unread one-time snippet overlap, exactly one receives `200` with the
+complete text and every other receives `404`. None receives a partial or corrupt body (FR-050).
+
+## Error body — added rows
+
+Shape unchanged: `{"error": "<message>"}` on every unsuccessful response (FR-069). Added and amended
+rows of feature 001's message table; as there, tests assert the status and a non-empty message rather
+than exact wording.
+
+| Status | Condition | Message |
+|--------|-----------|---------|
+| 400 | `burn_after_reading` present and not a boolean | `'burn_after_reading' must be true or false` |
+| 400 | `password` present and not a string of 1–128 characters | `'password' must be a string of 1 to 128 characters` |
+| 403 | protected snippet, password missing, repeated, undecodable, or wrong | `missing or invalid X-Paste-Password` |
+| 413 | request body over the read ceiling | `request body exceeds the maximum of 395776 bytes` *(the bound grew; see below)* |
+
+## The pre-read body ceiling
+
+`394,240` → **`395,776`** bytes (FR-071). The ceiling is still a fixed quantity, independent of any
+length the caller declares, so the memory protection it exists for is unchanged; it grew only because
+two new fields make a larger submission legitimately valid. Derivation and the measurement that shows
+the old bound really did reject a valid submission are in [../research.md](../research.md) R-09.
+
+This can only turn a previous rejection into an acceptance, never the reverse — the one permitted
+exception to FR-068. A submission whose `content` exceeds 65,536 encoded bytes is still a `413`, and
+the content limit itself is unchanged.
+
+A publication combining the largest valid `content`, a 128-character `password`, and
+`burn_after_reading` is accepted (FR-071).
+
+## Status codes used
+
+Unchanged set: `201`, `200`, `204`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `413`, `500`. This feature introduces no new
+status; it adds new routes to `400`, `403`, and `404`. No caller input this specification describes
+produces an unhandled failure or an unintended `500` (FR-070).
