+# Contract: On-disk storage format (feature 002 delta)
+
+**Feature**: `002-burn-after-reading-password` | **Plan**: [../plan.md](../plan.md)
+
+This file states **only what changes**. Feature 001's storage contract,
+[../../001-pastebin-service/contracts/storage.md](../../001-pastebin-service/contracts/storage.md),
+remains in force: a flat directory of `<id>.json` files, one JSON object per snippet, the same write
+protocol, the same read protocol, the same "damaged, never not-found" rule, and the same POSIX
+assumptions. The record gains two keys, the removal of a record gains a durability step, and a claim
+protocol is added for one-time snippets.
+
+This is still the **restart contract** (FR-047, FR-051, FR-056): a later instance started against
+the same directory must enforce the same one-time behaviour and require the same password. It is
+published here because tests depend on it to hand-write legacy and damaged records
+([../research.md](../research.md) R-10).
+
+## Record — two added keys
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg",
+  "content": "hello",
+  "created_at": "2026-10-23T20:15:00Z",
+  "expires_at": "2026-10-23T21:15:00Z",
+  "delete_token": "3bW9…",
+  "burn_after_reading": false,
+  "password": null
+}
+```
+
+Protected, one-time:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "7pL0nZ4cQw8xKdRm2vYbSa",
+  "content": "the secret",
+  "created_at": "2026-11-10T09:00:00Z",
+  "expires_at": null,
+  "delete_token": "Qx2…",
+  "burn_after_reading": true,
+  "password": {
+    "iterations": 215000,
+    "salt": "9f3c1a77b4e05d2886ac41be0f7d2355",
+    "hash": "1c0d…64 hex characters…"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+| Key | Required on read | Type | Rule |
+|-----|------------------|------|------|
+| `id`, `content`, `created_at`, `expires_at`, `delete_token` | **yes** | as feature 001 | unchanged |
+| `burn_after_reading` | no | boolean | Absent means `false` (FR-067). Any non-boolean value makes the record damaged. |
+| `password` | no | object or `null` | Absent or `null` means unprotected (FR-067). An object must have **exactly** `iterations`, `salt`, `hash`. |
+
+Inside `password`:
+
+| Key | Type | Rule |
+|-----|------|------|
+| `iterations` | integer | `> 0`. A JSON boolean is not an integer here, as elsewhere in this project. |
+| `salt` | string | Non-empty, even length, valid hexadecimal. 32 characters as written by this version (16 bytes). |
+| `hash` | string | Non-empty, even length, valid hexadecimal. 64 characters as written by this version (32 bytes). |
+
+A record is **valid** if it decodes as UTF-8 JSON, is an object, has all five required keys with the
+types feature 001 states, has no key outside the five required and the two optional ones, `id` equals
+the file-name stem, both timestamps parse under `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`, and both optional keys — where
+present — satisfy the rules above. Anything else is **damaged**: `500` for that identifier plus a
+stderr diagnostic, never `404` (FR-038, FR-039, carried forward).
+
+**This version always writes both optional keys**, with `burn_after_reading: false` and
+`password: null` for an ordinary snippet, so every record it creates has the same seven-key shape.
+The optional case exists solely for records written by the previous version (FR-067); nothing this
+version writes omits them.
+
+Keys outside the seven are still damaged, so feature 001's extra-key rule and its test are unaffected
+(FR-068).
+
+## The password is not stored
+
+`password` holds a salted, deliberately costly, one-way derivation and nothing else. The password's
+characters appear nowhere in the record, nowhere else in the directory, and on neither output stream
+(FR-056, FR-057, SC-015).
+
+```text
+salt       = secrets.token_bytes(16)
+hash       = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac("sha256", password.encode("utf-8"), salt, iterations, dklen=32)
+iterations = calibrated once per process so one verification costs ~50 ms on this host
+```
+
+Verification re-derives with **the record's own** `salt` and `iterations` — never the current
+process's calibration — and compares with `secrets.compare_digest` on the derived bytes, so a record
+written by a differently-calibrated process still verifies (FR-056) and the comparison reveals nothing
+about how much of a presented password was correct (FR-061). Rationale and the rejected alternatives
+are in [../research.md](../research.md) R-01 and R-02.
+
+Because the salt is drawn per snippet, two snippets with identical passwords have different `salt`
+and different `hash`, so one precomputed table cannot attack both (FR-073, SC-021).
+
+## Write protocol — unchanged
+
+Exactly feature 001's five steps: serialise, create `.tmp-<random>` exclusively, write, `flush`,
+`os.fsync`, close, `os.link` to `<id>.json` (retrying with a fresh identifier on `FileExistsError`),
+`os.unlink` the temporary, and only then send the `201`. The two added keys are part of the
+serialised record, so a reader still sees `<id>.json` either absent or complete, never partial
+(FR-043), and the one-time flag and the password derivation are durable before the publication is
+acknowledged (FR-047, FR-056).
+
+## Removal protocol — one added step
+
+Removing a record is now `os.unlink` of `<DIR>/<id>.json` followed by `os.fsync` of a descriptor
+opened on the storage directory:
+
+```text
+os.unlink(<DIR>/<id>.json)          # atomic; ENOENT if it is already gone
+fd = os.open(<DIR>, os.O_RDONLY)    # a directory-entry removal is otherwise only in the page cache
+os.fsync(fd); os.close(fd)
+```
+
+Both the `DELETE` endpoint and the one-time claim go through this one routine. The added `fsync`
+strengthens feature 001's FR-031 and changes nothing a caller can observe, so FR-068 holds; its cost
+measured 12.3 ms on this host.
+
+`FileNotFoundError` means something else removed the record first. Any other `OSError`, from either
+the `unlink` or the `fsync`, means storage is misbehaving and is reported as damaged storage — a
+`500` — which leaves a one-time snippet unconsumed as FR-049 requires.
+
+## Claim protocol (one-time snippets)
+
+The claim **is** the removal above. There is no tombstone, no claim file, no index, and no in-memory
+set of consumed identifiers.
+
+```text
+1. read and validate <id>.json                  → 404 if absent, 500 if damaged
+2. check expiry                                 → 404 if reached or passed (not a consumption)
+3. verify the password if the record is protected → 403 if it does not match (not a consumption)
+4. the text is now in memory
+5. remove the record durably (the routine above)
+     FileNotFoundError → another reader claimed it first → 404, serve nothing
+     other OSError     → 500, snippet unconsumed
+6. only now write the response, from the text held in memory
+```
+
+Step 5 is the whole guarantee. POSIX `unlink` removes a directory entry atomically and fails with
+`ENOENT` when it is already gone, so of any number of overlapping readers **exactly one** wins
+(FR-050; verified over 200 trials of sixteen racing threads in [../research.md](../research.md) R-04).
+The `fsync` in step 5 makes the claim durable **before** step 6 begins, which is FR-048's explicit
+ordering and FR-051's guarantee that a restart cannot resurrect a consumed snippet — including one
+whose response never reached its caller. Because the text was taken in step 4, the winner always has
+a complete body to send and a loser has nothing to send (FR-050).
+
+An expired or damaged one-time snippet never reaches step 5, so it is never consumed (FR-049,
+FR-053). A protected one never reaches step 5 without the correct password, so it cannot be burned by
+someone who cannot read it (SC-013).
+
+## Read protocol — unchanged
+
+One `read_bytes()` of `<DIR>/<id>.json`, then decode and validate. `FileNotFoundError` → `404`. Any
+other `OSError`, a decode failure, or a validation failure → `500`. Nothing is written on the read
+path except the claim in step 5 above, and only for a one-time snippet whose read has already
+succeeded.
+
+## Upgrade and downgrade
+
+**Upgrade** (previous version's records read by this one): supported and required. A five-key record
+is valid, reads as neither one-time nor protected, and is not damaged (FR-067).
+
+**Downgrade** (this version's records read by the previous one): not supported and not specified
+(spec Assumptions). The previous validator requires exactly five keys, so it would report a record
+written by this version as damaged.
